# No silent git restore

Never discard, overwrite, or resurrect working-tree or index state to clean up after yourself. If a command dirtied files you did not mean to touch, **stop and tell the user**. Do not "put it back."

## Forbidden unless the user named the exact command

- `git checkout --`
- `git checkout -- .` / `git checkout -- $(git diff …)`
- `git restore` / `git restore --staged` / `git restore --source`
- `git reset` / `git reset --hard` / `git reset --mixed`
- `git clean -f` / `git clean -fd`
- `git revert` (only when the user asked for a revert commit)
- `git stash` / `git stash drop` / `git stash pop` to hide or undo agent work
- piping `git diff --name-only` (or `git status`) into `xargs git checkout` / `git restore`

```text
# ❌ BAD — docs render / build / format touched extra files, so "undo the churn"
git diff --name-only | xargs git checkout --
git restore --source=HEAD -- path/that/was-already-dirty

# ✅ GOOD — leave the mess, report the paths, ask
# "project-docs.py render modified 200 READMEs. I did not restore them. How do you want to proceed?"
```

## Why

Restoring from HEAD or the index also wipes **the user's uncommitted work** on those same files, including work from other chats and the other clone. Staged-but-uncommitted files come back. Tracked edits disappear. That is not a cleanup; it is data loss.

## Allowed

- `git status`, `git diff`, `git log`, `git show` (read-only)
- `git add` / `git commit` only when the user asked to commit (follow the commit protocol)
- `git mv` when the user asked to move those paths

If you are unsure whether a git command writes the worktree or index, do not run it.
